Here's a **MinMaxStack** that tracks both minimum and maximum elements in O(1):

```java
class MinMaxStack {
    private static class Node {
        int value;
        int minSoFar;
        int maxSoFar;
    }

    private Node[] stack;
    private int size;

    public MinMaxStack(int capacity) {
        stack = new Node[capacity];
        size = 0;
    }

    public void push(int value) {
        if (size == stack.length) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Stack overflow");
        }

        Node node = new Node();
        node.value = value;
        node.minSoFar = (size == 0) ? value : Math.min(value, stack[size - 1].minSoFar);
        node.maxSoFar = (size == 0) ? value : Math.max(value, stack[size - 1].maxSoFar);

        stack[size++] = node;
    }

    public int pop() {
        if (size == 0) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Stack underflow");
        }
        return stack[--size].value;
    }

    public int getMin() {
        if (size == 0) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Stack is empty");
        }
        return stack[size - 1].minSoFar;
    }

    public int getMax() {
        if (size == 0) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Stack is empty");
        }
        return stack[size - 1].maxSoFar;
    }

    public int size() {
        return size;
    }
}
```

**The trick:** Each node caches the min and max seen *up to that point*. When you push, you compute the new min/max by comparing the incoming value against the previous top's cached extrema. Pop just returns the value—the cached bounds naturally disappear as the stack shrinks. All three operations (push, pop, getMin/getMax) are **O(1)** with **O(n)** space.

