Here's an interesting choice: **GeometricMeanStack** — tracks the geometric mean of all elements in O(1).

The geometric mean is more interesting than arithmetic mean (which is trivial with a running sum) because it typically requires logarithms. We'll avoid them via a clever trick: store the *product* of logs instead of the product itself.

```java
public class GeometricMeanStack {
    private static class Node {
        int value;
        double logSum;  // sum of log(abs(value)) for all elements in stack
        Node next;

        Node(int value, double logSum) {
            this.value = value;
            this.logSum = logSum;
        }
    }

    private Node top;
    private int size;

    public GeometricMeanStack() {
        this.top = null;
        this.size = 0;
    }

    /**
     * Push element onto stack in O(1).
     */
    public void push(int value) {
        if (value == 0) {
            // Geometric mean becomes 0 if any element is 0
            top = new Node(value, Double.NEGATIVE_INFINITY);
        } else {
            double newLogSum = (top == null) 
                ? Math.log(Math.abs(value))
                : top.logSum + Math.log(Math.abs(value));
            top = new Node(value, newLogSum);
        }
        size++;
    }

    /**
     * Pop element from stack in O(1).
     */
    public int pop() {
        if (top == null) {
            throw new EmptyStackException();
        }
        int value = top.value;
        top = top.next;
        size--;
        return value;
    }

    /**
     * Query geometric mean of all elements in O(1).
     * Returns the nth root of the product of absolute values.
     * Returns 0 if any element is 0; returns NaN if stack is empty.
     */
    public double geometricMean() {
        if (size == 0) {
            return Double.NaN;
        }
        if (top.logSum == Double.NEGATIVE_INFINITY) {
            return 0.0;  // A zero exists in the stack
        }
        // Geometric mean = exp(logSum / n)
        return Math.exp(top.logSum / size);
    }

    public int size() {
        return size;
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return size == 0;
    }
}
```

**Why this works:**
- **Product without overflow:** Instead of multiplying values (which overflows), we sum their logarithms: log(a·b·c) = log(a) + log(b) + log(c)
- **Nth root cheaply:** The geometric mean of n elements is the nth root of their product. Taking the nth root is equivalent to dividing the log sum by n, then exponentiating: n√(product) = exp(log(product)/n)
- **O(1) query:** Each node stores the cumulative log-sum up to that point, so `geometricMean()` just divides and exponentiates—constant time
- **O(1) push/pop:** We only update the top node; no traversal needed

**Example:**
```java
GeometricMeanStack stack = new GeometricMeanStack();
stack.push(1);
stack.push(8);
stack.push(27);
System.out.println(stack.geometricMean());  // ∛(1·8·27) = ∛216 = 6.0
stack.pop();
System.out.println(stack.geometricMean());  // √(1·8)

