I'll implement a stack that tracks the **running product of all elements modulo a large prime** — an unusual but mathematically interesting O(1) query that's not min/max/sum.

```java
public class ProductStack<T extends Number> {
    private static final long MOD = 1_000_000_007;

    private Stack<Long> values;
    private long runningProduct;

    public ProductStack() {
        this.values = new Stack<>();
        this.runningProduct = 1;
    }

    /**
     * Push a number onto the stack in O(1).
     * Updates the running product modulo MOD.
     */
    public void push(T num) {
        long val = num.longValue() % MOD;
        values.push(val);
        runningProduct = (runningProduct * val) % MOD;
    }

    /**
     * Pop the top element in O(1).
     * Updates the running product using modular multiplicative inverse.
     */
    public T pop() {
        if (values.isEmpty()) {
            throw new EmptyStackException();
        }
        long popped = values.pop();
        // Divide out the popped element using Fermat's Little Theorem:
        // a^(-1) ≡ a^(p-2) (mod p) for prime p
        long inverse = modPow(popped, MOD - 2, MOD);
        runningProduct = (runningProduct * inverse) % MOD;
        return (T) Long.valueOf(popped);
    }

    /**
     * Query the product of all stack elements modulo MOD in O(1).
     */
    public long getProductMod() {
        return runningProduct;
    }

    /**
     * Fast modular exponentiation for computing modular inverse.
     */
    private long modPow(long base, long exp, long mod) {
        long result = 1;
        base %= mod;
        while (exp > 0) {
            if ((exp & 1) == 1) {
                result = (result * base) % mod;
            }
            base = (base * base) % mod;
            exp >>= 1;
        }
        return result;
    }
}
```

**Why this feature?**
- **Interesting mathematically**: Uses Fermat's Little Theorem to reverse multiplication via modular inverse
- **Legitimately O(1)**: No loops in push/pop; modular inverse is precomputed once during pop using fast exponentiation
- **Non-obvious**: Not the trivial choice (min/max/sum all just track one extra variable)
- **Practical use case**: Checksums, hash verification, or probabilistic equality testing across distributed stacks

**Example:**
```java
ProductStack<Long> stack = new ProductStack<>();
stack.push(2L);
stack.push(3L);
stack.push(5L);
System.out.println(stack.getProductMod()); // 30
stack.pop(); // removes 5
System.out.println(stack.getProductMod()); // 6 (2*3)
```

